# Complexity

Let `N` be the size of the matrix.

### Time Complexity

Counting elements for one value takes:

```text
O(N)
```

Binary search takes:

```text
O(log(maxValue - minValue))
```

Therefore:

```text
O(N × log(maxValue - minValue))
```

Since `matrix[i][j] ≤ 10^5`, this is very efficient.

### Space Complexity

```text
O(1)
```

No additional array is created.

---

# Key Takeaway

This problem demonstrates an important **Binary Search on Answer** technique.

Instead of searching directly for the K-th element:

1. Guess an answer `mid`.
2. Count how many elements are `≤ mid`.
3. Use that count to decide which half to search.

The sorted rows and columns allow us to perform the counting step efficiently.

> **Pattern:** Binary Search on Answer + Sorted Matrix
